{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FrDFgJmK1BV2X7px154b7y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the information I need. Let me generate detailed worst-tier predictions for each planned experiment (plan_004 through plan_013).\n\nLet me think through each experiment carefully under the worst-tier assumptions:\n\n**Worst tier core assumptions:**\n1. New tasks show counterexamples — low Jaccard but CSS=1.0 faithfulness NOT better than single method (IOI is exception not rule)\n2. IFR and EAP-IG highly correlated (Jaccard > 0.6), K=4 effectively K≈2\n3. CSS faithfulness advantage highly dependent on ablation method (zero vs mean) and inseparable\n\n\n4. ACDC binarization problems recur in new forms on new tasks, top-k can't fully solve\n5. Insufficient data points to establish any statistically credible correlation\n\nLooking at the expected metric ranges across these experiments, I'm seeing very tight bounds: only 3-4 tasks total with 1-2 showing low Jaccard overlap, CSS advantages hovering near zero or slightly negative across different Jaccard regimes, and utility correlations that could swing anywhere from weakly negative to moderately negative depending on the task.\n\nFor the SVA experiment with K=3, I'm expecting localized circuits that should favor CSS, but the worst-case scenario is that even with low Jaccard similarity, CSS=1.0 still underperforms single methods—suggesting the circuit structure makes gradient agreement redundant rather than complementary. With DocQA/Factual, high Jaccard should predictably break CSS, though there's a chance the worst tier could show unexpected behavior where high Jaccard somehow doesn't fully suppress CSS performance, which would further muddy the correlation pattern I'm trying to establish.\n\nFor Qwen-2.5 0.5B IOI with three methods, IFR being from a different family should lower Jaccard, but worst-case is that IFR and EAP-IG end up too similar in practice, keeping Jaccard above 0.6 and making K=3 effectively behave like K=2. The Arithmetic task with IFR might not see enough Jaccard drop to save CSS. Scaling to Gemma-2 2B and Llama-3.1 8B for IOI could still hit the same binarization issues despite larger model capacity, with Llama-3.1 8B marked as a priority experiment.\n\nBeyond the core method comparisons, I'm considering whether binarization itself is the bottleneck—CSS might not stabilize across different K values, and the ablation method choice could flip which approach looks better, making them inseparable. CIRCUS negative ablation probably won't help since it already failed, and the Jaccard-CSS correlation might be too weak or point the wrong direction to be useful.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6900,"cache_read_input_tokens":21505,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6900},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"793f4d06-c5be-48a3-b889-ed9bd3449408","uuid":"9680039b-733d-4417-8e55-ab90d6917c07"}
